= Abstract
#v(4pt)

We model the interaction of two concurrent subdivision systems of sizes n and d
as the weighted sum of two periodic signals. As the balance between these
signals varies, the prominent peaks (*crests*) of the resulting waveform move
continuously along the cycle, tracing n smooth trajectories. A single
nontrivial resonance occurs when the two components are exactly balanced: all
crests align momentarily on a new rational grid, producing a highly structured
configuration.

At any parameter value, the ordered crest positions define a spacing vector
(the Huplet). In the limit of extreme modulation, this vector converges to a
canonical rotation of the Euclidean rhythm E(n,d), which we call the Quplet.
We show that this Euclidean pattern is selected naturally by the dynamics of
the crests and that the resulting Quplets form highly organised geometric
families, including symmetric twin pairs and affine lines in the rhythmic
simplex.